    "body": "> Indeed, I've thought about CSS-based scroll tracking also.\n> \n> Options:\n> \n> 1. drop the scripting check for lazy-loading and let browsers introduce a separate preference for scroll tracking protection.\n> 2. also disable various CSS features when scripting is disabled.>\n\n... Including implementation specific lazy loading of background images. \nThis might be detrimental perf-wise for some pages.\n\n> I assume (2) is not web-compatible for sandboxed iframes.\n\nYea it would probably break same-origin iframes with scripting disabled if this options is used as a security feature rather than a tracking prevention feature.",
